Relevant compost section:
   200	  12.6 % of initial OM as CH₄ in static swine piles vs 0.5 %/0.5 % turned** —
   201	  ~20× worse never turning. So "turning increases emissions" applies between
   202	  turning *frequencies* on managed piles, not vs. total neglect. Actionable
   203	  schedule (Ahn 2011 via p. 233): **avoid turning in the early phase while
   204	  O₂/T are in range; turn later when pile O₂ has recovered**, so formed CH₄
   205	  gets oxidised in-pile — directly drivable by our O₂/CH₄ channels. Each
   206	  turning also vents stored pore gas as a burst (8–12 % of total CH₄ in home
   207	  units, Andersen 2010a) — spot measurements right after turning are doubly
   208	  meaningless (cf. the O₂-collapse note in `COMPOST-METROLOGY.md` §4).
   209	  **Plant-scale evidence that the trade-off can net out either way** (Hrad
   210	  2014, inspected): a *daily*-turned biowaste plant still achieved a very low
   211	  annual EF (112–940 g CH₄/Mg) even though **active operating states were
   212	  higher as a group** — up to 95 % above standstill, passive states 38 %
   213	  below active overall (a group-level result: individual turning periods
   214	  occasionally sat *below* the same day's morning standstill, Table 2,
   215	  pp. 2448–2451) — and, counterintuitively, **daily turning suppressed the
   216	  convective chimney flow**: core O₂ sat < 5 % through the turning week but
   217	  recovered to 10–17 % over turn-free weekends, with Monday-morning fluxes
   218	  > 2× the post-turning level (Fig. 6, Table 2). Across a year of ~3400
   219	  staff pore-gas measurements and seven whole-site campaigns, **no
   220	  correlation between core concentrations and whole-site flux could be
   221	  established** (p. 2451; a qualitative conclusion — the flux side has only
   222	  seven campaign days, no 3400-pair statistic) — still the clearest
   223	  statement that our probes measure process state, not emissions.
   224	
   225	### 1.3 Pore-gas concentrations — the ranges a sensor must cover
   226	
   227	| Gas | In-pile range (measured) | Source |
   228	|---|---|---|
   229	| CH₄ | mean 6.7 vol %, **max 44 vol %** in windrow centre (Andersen 2010b Table 3); up to **47 %** (470 mL/L at 1.5 m depth, centre of 14-week household-waste heaps; heap mean 34 mL/L ≈ 3.4 %; small heaps ≤ 0.2 % — Beck-Friis 2000 Table IV, **now inspected from primary**, `literature/emissions/`); **37–45 % at 1 m depth** in digestate windrows (Nguyen & Cuhls 2016, inspected); ≤ 1.2 % in 3-m green-waste (Hellebrand 1998, second-hand); manure windrows (Hao 2001, now inspected): turned < 15 % at the bottom / 0–3 % in the top 46 cm, but the **unturned (passive) windrow reached 53 % CH₄ at 107 cm on day 97** (p. 379) — scales with pile size and falls with turning | inspected + second-hand |
   230	| CO₂ | mean ≈ 17.7 %, up to 45 % (Andersen 2010b; Nguyen & Cuhls); **up to ~75 vol %** in the core of daily-turned 1.5-m biowaste windrows (Hrad 2014 Fig. 5, inspected — graphical reading of a scatter plot, axis 0–80 %) — **above the K33 ICB's 0–30 % range**: in a badly anaerobic core the reading is *out of specified range* — overrange behavior (clamp / error / nonlinear / slow recovery) is undocumented, same open point as the CH₄ NDIRs (§3.1): bench-test before interpreting full-scale as "≥ 30 %" | inspected |
   231	| O₂ | 12–19 % early → 4–7 % (can hit 0 at 1 m) | Andersen 2010b; Nguyen & Cuhls |
   232	| N₂O | ppm-level to low-‰: mean 138 ppmv (Andersen Table 3); 20–550 ppmv at 30–107 cm (Hao via Brown); **1–3421 µL N₂O-N/L (up to ~3400 ppmv)** in household-waste heaps (Beck-Friis 2000 abstract + Table III, inspected) — the top of the range exceeds even the Dynament 0–1000 ppm span (§3.3) | inspected |
   233	| H₂S | tens of ppm in early sludge exhaust; **89–2083 ppm** 10-s peaks disturbing anaerobically-stored spent mushroom compost (Velusami 2013, inspected). No systematic pore-profile study for ordinary green-waste windrows was found — data gap, not absence | partial/inspected |
   234	
   235	Design consequences: CH₄ needs a **0–5 % (or wider) vol range**, not ppm; N₂O
   236	needs ppm resolution (→ no affordable sensor found, §3.3); an H₂S cell should
   237	tolerate > 1000 ppm transients if material is ever stored anaerobically.
   238	Also: **O₂ alone is ambiguous** — Nguyen & Cuhls measured 0 % O₂ + 20 % CO₂ in
   239	still-aerobic material; CH₄ ≈ 0 was what disambiguated it. That is the
   240	argument for adding CH₄ to the LOX-02 + K33 line. (One-directional evidence:
   241	high CH₄ confirms methanogenesis; absent CH₄ does not confirm the pocket is
   242	aerobic.) Hrad 2014 (pp. 2449–2450, inspected) supplies a ready-made
   243	**classification rule for the CO₂+CH₄ pair**: CO₂ ≈ 15.8 vol % corresponds
   244	to the point where the stipulated 5 % minimum O₂ is still retained, and
   245	≈ 20.8 vol % to theoretical *complete* O₂ consumption; **high CO₂ + low CH₄
   246	= acetogenic pre-phase, high CO₂ + high CH₄ = established methanogenesis**
   247	("pre-phase = still recoverable by aeration" is our operational inference,
   248	not Hrad's claim); in their plant, core CH₄ > 2 vol % occurred mostly
   249	*before* turning.
   250	
   251	---
   252	
   253	## 2. Mitigation levers your sensors can actually drive
   254	
   255	- **Aeration is the master CH₄ lever — but not monotonically good**: keep

