   101	## Datasheet basis
   102	
   103	**TI TMP117, SNOSD82D rev D, JUNE 2018 – REVISED SEPTEMBER 2022.** Read as a PDF
   104	and, for the package drawings, rendered and read off the image — not from the
   105	text layer, which mis-paired the dimension callouts. Nothing below is quoted from
   106	memory.
   107	
   108	| fact | source |
   109	|---|---|
   110	| ADD0 connects to GND, V+, SDA or SCL | §5, Table 5-1, p.4 |
   111	| **Addresses: GND→0x48, V+→0x49, SDA→0x4A, SCL→0x4B** | **§7.5.3.1, Table 7-2, p.21** |
   112	| ALERT is an open-drain output needing a pullup if used | §5, Table 5-1 |
   113	| Accuracy ±0.1 °C max, 25–50 °C, **"Thermal Pad unsoldered (DRV Package)"** | §6.5, p.5 |
   114	| Resolution 7.8125 m°C; long-term drift ±0.03 °C / 1000 h @150 °C | §6.5 |
   115	| RθJA 70.7, RθJC(bot) 11.7, RθJB 35.4 K/W; thermal mass 5.1 mJ/°C | §6.4, p.5 |
   116	| **100 nF bypass is *required*, as close to V+/GND as possible** | §9, p.35 |
   117	| **"The package thermal pad is not connected to the device ground… If the thermal pad is soldered, it must be left floating or grounded."** | §9, p.35 |
   118	| Solid-surface layout: minimal thickness, bottom copper plane, bottom mask removed + gold, thermal paste, minimal top copper, insulate the sensor | §10.1 item 2, p.36 |
   119	| "In some cases, the pullup resistor can be the heat source… maintain some distance" | §10.1, p.36 |
   120	| Exposed pad **1.0 ± 0.1 (X) × 1.6 ± 0.1 (Y)**, chamfered at pin 1 | p.40, drawing 4223922/A |
   121	| Land pattern: **6 pads, no EP land**; 0.45 × 0.30, 0.65 pitch, 1.95 columns, R0.05 | p.41 |
   122	
   123	`TMP117AIDRVR` is LCSC **C699536**, WSON-6 2×2, 3558 in stock at $0.98 when
   124	checked (2026-08-07), JLCPCB **extended** part — a setup fee applies.
   125	
   126	---
   127	
   128	## The decisions, and who made them
   129	
   130	Four were the user's, taken before any placement: **solder the thermal pad to a
   131	floating island**, **JLCPCB PCBA**, **solder pads for flying leads**, **stick with
   132	a narrow neck**. The rest are recorded here as decisions, not left to emerge from
   133	the layout.
   134	
   135	### Soldering the exposed pad — a documented departure from TI
   136	
   137	TI's recommended land pattern (p.41) has **six pads and no EP land at all**, and
   138	§9 says to leave the thermal pad unsoldered; their ±0.1 °C accuracy line is
   139	specified with exactly that condition.
   140	
   141	**TI's §10.1 Note is more adverse than that, and says so specifically about rigid
   142	boards:** *"To achieve a high precision temperature reading for a rigid PCB, do
   143	not solder down the thermal pad. For a flexible PCB, the user can solder the
   144	thermal pad…"*, and item 1 adds *"For cases when system calibration is not
   145	planned, TI recommends not soldering the thermal pad."* This is rigid 0.6 mm FR4,
   146	so the Note applies to us and TI does **not** endorse this choice for this board.
   147	
   148	It is still the right trade for a probe measuring a solid surface tens of kelvin
   149	above ambient — item 1's own rationale, that soldering "minimizes the thermal
   150	resistance to the PCB, reduces the response time as temperature changes and
   151	minimizes the temperature offset between the device and measured object", is
   152	exactly what this design needs — but it is taken **against** TI's recommendation
   153	for rigid boards, and it is conditional on calibrating the built probe.
   154	The cost is the package mechanical stress §10.1 item 1 warns about, which is why
   155	reflow (not hand soldering) is specified.
   156	
   157	TI gives no land geometry for the soldered case, so the EP land is the package's
   158	own nominal 1.0 × 1.6 mm — never oversized. The gap from the EP land to the
   159	nearest terminal land is **0.250 mm**, and that figure is set by the package, not
   160	by us; `audit.py` measures it so a future edit cannot quietly eat into it.
   161	
   162	§9 also requires a soldered thermal pad to be *floating or grounded*. It is
   163	**floating** here (net `TPAD`): it is thermally bonded to the shunt, and grounding
   164	it would drag the I²C ground to shunt potential.
   165	
   166	### Layer count: 2
   167	
   168	Five static/low-speed signals at ~200 µA have no use for inner planes, and on a
   169	0.6 mm board an inner plane would sit ~0.2 mm under the sensor island and
   170	short-circuit the thermal isolation the neck exists to create.
   171	
   172	### Board thickness: 0.6 mm, ENIG
   173	
   174	SNOSD82D §10.1 item 2 — "Use PCB with minimal thickness" — and "Remove bottom
   175	solder mask and cover exposed copper with gold layer if possible". The stackup is
   176	written explicitly into the `.kicad_pcb` and verified to survive a KiCad
   177	load/save round-trip; without it the fab builds its house stack and every
   178	dielectric-dependent number below is unbacked.
   179	
   180	### Address straps on the island, not at the connector
   181	
   182	Table 5-1 allows ADD0 → **SDA** and **V+**, and those are pins 6 and 5 — the same
   183	package face as ADD0 (pin 4), 1.3 mm and 0.65 mm away. Strapping to those instead
   184	of to GND keeps the entire address network on the sensor island: no fifth neck
   185	lane, no wire-end congestion, and the connector stays at four wires.
   186	
   187	- **R3 fitted → ADD0 = SDA → 0x4A** (default build)
   188	- **R4 fitted → ADD0 = V+ → 0x49** (second probe on the same bus)
   189	
   190	Fit exactly one. **ADD0 must never float** — the address would be undefined. Both
   191	are 0 Ω and carry no current, so unlike the bus pullups §10.1 warns about they are
   192	not a heat source beside the sensor.
   193	
   194	### No bus pullups on the board
   195	
   196	§10.1: "the pullup resistor can be the heat source, therefore maintain some
   197	distance between the resistor and the device." On a probe measuring a surface, a
   198	pullup on the sensor island is a heater bolted to the thing being measured. The
   199	host supplies them.
   200	
   201	### ALERT is not routed
   202	
   203	Open-drain data-ready / over-temperature output. Bringing it out costs a fifth
   204	neck lane and a fifth wire, and the brief is a four-wire probe. It is available on
   205	the die for a future revision; the pad is on the board but connects to nothing.
   206	
   207	### One deliberate layer change
   208	
   209	ADD0 leaves the package's east face and the V+ leg to C1 crosses that exit — pin 5
   210	sits directly above pin 4 on the same face, so on one layer the two paths cannot
   211	both exist. **ADD0** takes a short B.Cu hop, not VDD.
   212	
   213	Which net hops matters, and so does where. B.Cu here is the thermal-contact pour,
   214	and a hop cuts a ~0.7 mm slot through it. This slot runs along y = 4.5 mm, *south*
   215	of the die and 2.6 mm south of the six thermal vias, which are all north of the
   216	package — so it sits nowhere on the path from the shunt contact to those vias.
   217	Routing VDD instead was tried and is worse: its legs to both C1 and R4 put vias in
   218	the middle of the contact area, where bare copper on two different nets meets a
   219	shunt bedded in thermal compound. DRC called that `solder_mask_bridge`, and it is a
   220	real short, not a formality.
   221	
   222	---
   223	
   224	## Thermal design, with the numbers
   225	
   226	Measured by `audit.py` against the real geometry, not asserted here.
   227	
   228	```
   229	  shunt ──[ TIM 2.2 K/W ]── island ──[ EP+vias+copper 34 K/W ]── die ──[ ? ]── air
   230	                              │
   231	                              └──[ neck copper 357 K/W ]── wire end ── air
   232	```
   233	
   234	| quantity | value | how |
   235	|---|---|---|
   236	| bottom contact area | **45.9 mm²** (47.0 on `m3`) | mask apertures ∩ actual B.Cu TPAD fill |
   237	| → TIM resistance | 2.2 K/W (2.1 on `m3`) | 100 µm of 1 W/m·K compound |
   238	| **EP → bottom island** | **34 K/W** | Dijkstra over the real TPAD copper graph, 6/6 vias |
   239	| neck copper | 4 traces × 0.15 mm → **357 K/W** | measured on a cut across the neck |
   240	| **island leak fraction** | **0.61 %** | neck 2.80 mW/K vs TIM 459 mW/K |
   241	| decoupling loop | 4.45 mm (VDD) + 4.44 mm (GND) | real copper, walked track by track |
   242	
   243	**The island tracks the shunt to 99.4 % of its rise above ambient.** That part is
   244	solid, and it is the part the layout controls.
   245	
   246	### The exposed-pad path is copper-limited, not via-limited
   247	
   248	An earlier revision of this document claimed **10.6 K/W** for the exposed-pad
   249	path, from six via barrels in parallel. That was wrong: the vias sit 0.6–1.3 mm
   250	*north* of the EP land and everything between them is a strip of 35 µm copper.
   251	The 1.0 mm wide, 0.6 mm long stub out of the EP land alone is 0.6 squares at
   252	**71.4 K/W per square = 43 K/W**, four times the whole via array, and the barrels
   253	are only 64 K/W each in parallel behind it. Walking the actual copper graph gives
   254	**34 K/W** end to end.
   255	
   256	That stub width is package-set: SNOSD82D p.40/41 leaves only 1.0 mm between the
   257	terminal lands, so ~40 K/W of this is not removable on a rigid 2-layer board.
   258	`audit.py` now measures the graph rather than counting vias — a narrowed stub
   259	used to degrade the real path ~7× with both the guard and DRC still green.
   260	
   261	### No k figure is claimed any more
   262	
   263	The earlier revision quoted `k = 0.76` bare / `0.93` insulated from
   264	`RθJA / (RθJA + down-path)`. Two things are wrong with that and they push in
   265	opposite directions:
   266	
   267	- the down-path omitted the 34 K/W of copper entirely (it used 11 K/W, not 46);
   268	- **RθJA = 70.7 K/W is a JEDEC 2s2p board-in-still-air composite that already
   269	  contains a board path** — using it as the parallel die-to-air branch
   270	  double-counts the very board this design replaces with a shunt. The metric that
   271	  describes the top path is RθJC(top) = **82.3 K/W** (§6.4), but the convection
   272	  off ~4 mm² of package top behind it is tens of thousands of K/W, which would
   273	  say k ≈ 1.
   274	
   275	The honest statement: **the down-path is measurable (45.7 K/W including
   276	RθJC(bot)); the up-path is not, by hand, in this geometry.** Anyone fitting a TCR
   277	from this probe should calibrate the built board against a reference rather than
   278	trusting a computed k. Insulating the top of the package per SNOSD82D §10.1
   279	item 2 still helps and still costs nothing — it just cannot be quantified here.
   280	
   281	## Variants
   282	
   283	`variants.py` is the only place a variant is allowed to differ. Both boards come
   284	out of the same `gen_sch.py` / `gen_pcb.py` / `audit.py`, so a fix to the
   285	routing, the mask apertures or the thermal path lands on both and they cannot
   286	drift the way a copied directory would. The `m3` board changes the outline and
   287	adds `H1`; **nothing else moves** — same placement, same routing, same mask
   288	apertures, same neck — so every thermal number in this document holds for both.
   289	
   290	### `m3` — M3 clearance hole for screwing to a heatsink
   291	
   292	A 6 mm tab on the **north** end, hole at (4.50, −2.80), 3.2 mm NPTH. Board
   293	becomes 9.7 × 21.0 mm.
   294	
   295	**Why north, and why that far out.** The screw head has to bear on the board and
   296	the TMP117 is 0.8 mm tall, so the head cannot overlap the package. A 5.5 mm
   297	DIN 912 cap head needs its centre ≥ 2.75 mm clear of the package body at
   298	y = 2.0, and further still to clear the top-side thermal copper, which reaches
   299	y = 0.55. That puts the centre at y = −2.80 and the die **5.8 mm from the screw**.
   300	Closer is only possible by moving the six thermal vias out of the north of the
   301	island, which would rebuild the one path this whole design is about — not worth
   302	1.5 mm on a 0.6 mm laminate that conforms anyway.
   303	
   304	**The tab carries no copper, on either face.** This is the part that matters
   305	electrically. The bottom island is bare `TPAD` copper floating at whatever the
   306	shunt floats at, and the screw goes into the heatsink, so it sits at the
   307	mounting surface's potential. Copper under the head — or a plated barrel — would
   308	bond the two and **silently bypass the user's thermal interface, which is the
   309	only isolation this board has**. So the hole is unplated, its pad has no annular
   310	ring, and the tab is plain laminate under solder mask.
   311	
   312	Measured on the generated board, not asserted here:
   313	
   314	| quantity | measured | needed |
   315	|---|---|---|
   316	| copper-free radius about the hole | **3.100 mm** | ≥ 3.0 (6.0 mm keep-out for a 5.5 mm head) |
   317	| `TPAD` → screw creepage, hole wall to copper | **1.50 mm** | vs 0.25 mm `TPAD`→signal elsewhere on this board |
   318	| largest head that seats fully on the board | **6.41 mm** | ≥ 5.5 mm (DIN 912 cap) |
   319	| bottom contact area | **47.0 mm²** | ≥ 30; **+1.1 mm² vs base**, see below |
   320	
   321	The contact area is the one thermal number the tab does move, and upward. On the
   322	base board the north board edge sits at y = 0, and the pour's fill is clipped to
   323	0.501 mm from it by `min_copper_edge_clearance`, short of the zone outline at
   324	0.300. On `m3` that edge is 6 mm away, so the fill reaches its own outline and
   325	the aperture gains a 0.10 × 8.7 mm strip. Measured fill: 50.5 mm² base,
   326	52.3 mm² m3. Nothing was designed for this; it is a side effect of the outline
   327	and is recorded because the number differs between the two boards.
   328	
   329	**Use a DIN 912 cap screw, no washer.** A DIN 125 flat washer is 7.0 mm across
   330	and would overhang the 6.0 mm copper-free zone. If the mounting surface is live
   331	and you need the screw isolated from it too, that is a shoulder washer and a
   332	nylon screw — this board gives you 1.5 mm of laminate, not a rated barrier.
   333	
   334	**What the tab does to the thermal contact.** The tab's bottom is mask over
   335	laminate; the island's is 35 µm of copper plus ENIG. The island therefore stands
   336	~20–25 µm proud of the tab, so the screw pulls the tab down and the island still
   337	bears against the heatsink. Front silkscreen only on the tab — ink on its back
   338	face would be stand-off in exactly the wrong place.
   339	
   340	---
   341	
   342	## Isolation — read this before energising
   343	
   344	**This board provides no rated isolation barrier, and none is claimed.**
   345	
   346	The `TPAD` net — exposed pad, six vias, and the entire bare bottom face — floats
   347	at whatever the shunt body sits at if the user's thermal interface fails. The
   348	binding on-board clearance from `TPAD` to the signal nets is **0.250 mm**, set by
   349	the TMP117 package itself (EP land to terminal land) and not improvable. The
   350	`TPAD` net class is set to that same 0.25 mm rather than to some larger figure the
   351	geometry cannot achieve.
   352	
   353	The barrier is the **user's insulating thermal interface**, external to this
   354	board, as agreed at the outset. `TP2` exists so that barrier can be ohm-checked
   355	against the probe's own ground after the board is mounted and before anything is
   356	energised — by then the bottom face is buried in compound and unreachable. The
   357	back silkscreen says `TPAD FLOATS`.
   358	
   359	---
   360	
   361	## Assembly and build notes
   362	
   363	1. **Reflow, not hand soldering.** §10.1 item 1: manual soldering adds mechanical
   364	   stress to the package and degrades precision. JLCPCB PCBA; `TMP117AIDRVR` is an
   365	   extended part.
   366	2. **Fit exactly one of R3 / R4.** Default build is R3 → **0x4A** (Table 7-2,
   367	   p.21). This line said 0x4B until 2026-08-07: the review's address fix swept
   368	   the generators, the BOM and the silkscreen but missed the assembly
   369	   instruction, which is the one a person actually follows.
   370	3. **Bed the bottom island to the shunt with electrically insulating thermally
   371	   conductive compound.** The bottom is bare ENIG over ~46 mm² (47 on `m3`), with six open
   372	   0.3 mm via barrels in it — use compound, not a solid pad, and expect some
   373	   pump-through: re-bed between runs rather than assuming the joint is stable.
   374	4. **Cover the top of the TMP117 with insulating foam or tape.** This is worth more
   375	   than everything else on this list — see the k figures above.
   376	5. **Ohm-check TP2 against the GND wire pad before energising.**
   377	6. Pullups live at the host, not here.
   378	7. Keep the probe out of moving air; §10.1 item 2 asks for minimal air movement
   379	   and horizontal surfaces.
   380	8. **Host pullups must be > 2 kΩ** — §8.2.2.2, p.34: "Avoid using small-value
   381	   pullup resistors on the SDA and ALERT pins."
   382	9. The ±0.1 °C accuracy figure is specified at **8 averages, 1 Hz conversion
   383	   cycle**, with I²C input levels **VIL ≤ 0.05·V+ and VIH ≥ 0.95·V+** (§6.5) —
   384	   tighter than the 0.3/0.7·V+ logic thresholds. Both are host/firmware
   385	   obligations, not board ones.
   386	10. **Calibrate the built probe against a reference** before trusting absolute ΔT
   387	   — see the thermal section.
   388	11. **`m3` only: M3 cap screw, no washer, and do not over-torque.** The board is
   389	    0.6 mm FR4 with 1.8 mm of material between the hole and the north edge. The
   390	    screw's job is to hold the island flat against the surface, not to clamp
   391	    hard; the compound sets the bond line. A washer larger than 6.0 mm overhangs
   392	    the copper-free zone and can reach `TPAD`.
   393	
   394	## Connector
   395	
   396	| J1 pad | net | notes |
   397	|---|---|---|
   398	| 1 | SCL | |
   399	| 2 | GND | probe ground; **not** the shunt/TPAD potential |
   400	| 3 | V+ | **1.8–5.5 V** host-supplied (1.7 V min applies only to Tᴀ ≤ 70 °C, §6.3; this probe runs hotter). Abs max 6 V (§6.1) |
   401	| 4 | SDA | |
   402	
   403	Pad order is not arbitrary: it is the order the nets arrive down the neck, so
   404	nothing crosses between the island and the connector. Labels are on the front
   405	silkscreen, staggered into two rows because a three-character label at the
   406	legible-stroke minimum is wider than the 1.8 mm pad pitch.
