   339	
   340	---
   341	
   342	## Isolation — read this before energising
   343	
   344	**This board provides no rated isolation barrier, and none is claimed.**
   345	
   346	The `TPAD` net — exposed pad, six vias, and the entire bare bottom face — floats
   347	at whatever the shunt body sits at if the user's thermal interface fails. The
   348	binding on-board clearance from `TPAD` to the signal nets is **0.250 mm**, set by
   349	the TMP117 package itself (EP land to terminal land) and not improvable. The
   350	`TPAD` net class is set to that same 0.25 mm rather than to some larger figure the
   351	geometry cannot achieve.
   352	
   353	The barrier is the **user's insulating thermal interface**, external to this
   354	board, as agreed at the outset. `TP2` exists so that barrier can be ohm-checked
   355	against the probe's own ground after the board is mounted and before anything is
   356	energised — by then the bottom face is buried in compound and unreachable. The
   357	back silkscreen says `TPAD FLOATS`.
   358	
   359	---
   360	
   361	## Assembly and build notes
   362	
   363	1. **Reflow, not hand soldering.** §10.1 item 1: manual soldering adds mechanical
   364	   stress to the package and degrades precision. JLCPCB PCBA; `TMP117AIDRVR` is an
   365	   extended part.
   366	2. **Fit exactly one of R3 / R4.** Default build is R3 → **0x4A** (Table 7-2,
   367	   p.21). This line said 0x4B until 2026-08-07: the review's address fix swept
   368	   the generators, the BOM and the silkscreen but missed the assembly
   369	   instruction, which is the one a person actually follows.
   370	3. **Bed the bottom island to the shunt with electrically insulating thermally
   371	   conductive compound.** The bottom is bare ENIG over ~46 mm² (47 on `m3`), with six open
   372	   0.3 mm via barrels in it — use compound, not a solid pad, and expect some
   373	   pump-through: re-bed between runs rather than assuming the joint is stable.
   374	4. **Cover the top of the TMP117 with insulating foam or tape.** This is worth more
   375	   than everything else on this list — see the k figures above.
   376	5. **Ohm-check TP2 against the GND wire pad before energising.**
   377	6. Pullups live at the host, not here.
   378	7. Keep the probe out of moving air; §10.1 item 2 asks for minimal air movement
   379	   and horizontal surfaces.
   380	8. **Host pullups must be > 2 kΩ** — §8.2.2.2, p.34: "Avoid using small-value
   381	   pullup resistors on the SDA and ALERT pins."
   382	9. The ±0.1 °C accuracy figure is specified at **8 averages, 1 Hz conversion
   383	   cycle**, with I²C input levels **VIL ≤ 0.05·V+ and VIH ≥ 0.95·V+** (§6.5) —
   384	   tighter than the 0.3/0.7·V+ logic thresholds. Both are host/firmware
   385	   obligations, not board ones.
   386	10. **Calibrate the built probe against a reference** before trusting absolute ΔT
   387	   — see the thermal section.
   388	11. **`m3` only: M3 cap screw, no washer, and do not over-torque.** The board is
   389	    0.6 mm FR4 with 1.8 mm of material between the hole and the north edge. The
   390	    screw's job is to hold the island flat against the surface, not to clamp
   391	    hard; the compound sets the bond line. A washer larger than 6.0 mm overhangs
   392	    the copper-free zone and can reach `TPAD`.
   393	
   394	## Connector
   395	
   396	| J1 pad | net | notes |
   397	|---|---|---|
   398	| 1 | SCL | |
   399	| 2 | GND | probe ground; **not** the shunt/TPAD potential |
   400	| 3 | V+ | **1.8–5.5 V** host-supplied (1.7 V min applies only to Tᴀ ≤ 70 °C, §6.3; this probe runs hotter). Abs max 6 V (§6.1) |
   401	| 4 | SDA | |
   402	
   403	Pad order is not arbitrary: it is the order the nets arrive down the neck, so
   404	nothing crosses between the island and the connector. Labels are on the front
   405	silkscreen, staggered into two rows because a three-character label at the
   406	legible-stroke minimum is wider than the 1.8 mm pad pitch.
   407	
   408	## BOM
   409	
   410	| ref | value | MPN | LCSC | fitted |
   411	|---|---|---|---|---|
   412	| U1 | TMP117 | TMP117AIDRVR (TI) | C699536 | yes |
   413	| C1 | 100 n 16 V X7R 0402 | CL05B104KO5NNNC (Samsung) | C1525 | yes |
   414	| R3 | 0 Ω 0402 | RC0402JR-070RL (YAGEO) | C25099 | **yes** → 0x4A |
   415	| R4 | 0 Ω 0402 | RC0402JR-070RL (YAGEO) | C25099 | **no** → 0x49 |
   416	| J1 | wire pads | — | — | bare copper |
   417	| TP2 | test pad | — | — | bare copper |
   418	| H1 | M3 3.2 mm NPTH | — | — | `m3` variant only; excluded from the BOM |
   419	
   420	## What has *not* been done
   421	
   422	- **No board has been built or measured.** Every thermal figure above is computed
   423	  from datasheet thermal metrics and geometry. The 300 K/W insulated-top estimate
   424	  in particular is a guess with the right order of magnitude, not a measurement.
   425	- No 3D/mechanical check against any specific shunt body.
   426	- Gerbers and drill files are exported to `fab/` (base) and `fab-m3/` but have
   427	  not been reviewed in a gerber viewer.
   428	- **`m3`: the clamping mechanics are reasoned, not measured.** The screw is
   429	  5.8 mm from the die and the board is a 0.6 mm cantilever over that span. The
   430	  argument that the bond line stays thin — 0.6 mm FR4 is floppy and the compound
   431	  fills — is plausible and untested. If the built probe reads low against a
   432	  reference, the bond line under the die is the first place to look.
   433	- `m3`: no thread-engagement or torque figure. The board is a clearance hole; the
   434	  fastener and its seat are the user's.
   435	
