   463	
   464	### Open — your call
   465	
   466	| # | finding | why it is not fixed here |
   467	|---|---|---|
   468	| A | *(you chose to ignore this)* **9.7 mm is below JLCPCB's 10 × 10 mm Economic PCBA minimum**, and there are no rails, tooling edge or fiducials. As generated the board cannot be assembled. | Fixing it means widening to ≥10 mm or panelising. The board is already 0.35 mm off-centre (`CX = 4.50` vs a true centreline of 4.85), so 10.0 mm fixes both — but it is a size change you should sign off. |
   469	| D | **0.25 mm TPAD-to-signal on B.Cu is *not* package-set** — that one is `zone.SetLocalClearance(0.25)` plus the choice to route the ADD0 hop through the pour. Only the F.Cu 0.25 mm is forced by the package. IPC-2221 B1 gives 0.13 mm to 100 V and 0.4 mm above it, so 100 V is the cliff edge, not a margin. | The doc now over-claims; the geometry is fine at ≤100 V but has no headroom. |
   470	| E | **J1's "bare copper" pads carry solder paste** (4 × 1.8 mm² of stencil aperture). TP2 and U1.7 correctly do not. | Pre-tinned pads may be what you want, but right now it is accidental. |
   471	| F | **`neck_conductance()` sees only perfectly vertical `PCB_TRACK`s** — a diagonal trace, an arc, or a pour extended down the neck is invisible, and the verdict moves toward PASS as the geometry gets worse. `y_cut = 8.5` and `ISLAND_Y/NECK_Y` are also hand-copied from `gen_pcb.py` with nothing checking they still match. | Real anti-monotone hole; the current board happens to be all-vertical. |
   472	| G | **EP paste coverage 65 %** — high for a QFN thermal pad (50–60 % typical), and voiding here degrades the one path that matters. | |
   473	| H | Four sharp 90° internal corners at the neck root on 0.6 mm FR4; a router cannot cut them sharp and the fillet eats the 0.275 mm copper-to-edge at (3.70, 10.0–10.5). | |
   474	| I | Silk is at exactly the 0.8 mm / 0.15 mm floor with zero margin; `TPAD FLOATS` is on the **back**, i.e. the face being cemented down, while TP2 — the exposed hazard-net pad — is on the front with no marking. | |
   475	| J | `gen_pcb.py`'s module docstring still says "7.0 × 14.0 mm … neck 2.0 mm wide, five traces". The constants are right; the prose a reviewer reads first is wrong about every number. | |
   476	| K | With V+ open and the host bus alive, U1 is phantom-powered through the host pullups into its ESD clamps, plus a second DC path SDA → R3 → ADD0. A broken supply lead is a likely fault on a flying-lead probe. No series resistors or clamps anywhere. | |
   477	
   478	### How B and C were fixed
   479	
   480	The bottom mask aperture is **three rectangles that merge into one region with a
   481	single notch**, rather than one plain rectangle:
   482	
   483	```
   484	                    A                            y 0.40 .. 3.85
   485	      B   |  keepout: ADD0 B.Cu hop  |  C        y 3.85 .. 6.60
   486	```
   487	
   488	The keep-out is open to the board edge, so it is not an isolated island of solder
   489	mask that could lift — the exported gerber is a single region.
   490	
   491	- **Mask web to the ADD0 hop: 0.175 → 0.325 mm.** `solder_mask_min_width` is now
   492	  0.25 mm (JLCPCB's green minimum) instead of KiCad's default 0.0, and
   493	  `pad_to_mask_clearance` is 0.05 mm — NSMD, which SNOSD82D p.41 marks
   494	  PREFERRED at "0.07 MAX ALL AROUND". Flush mask edges, which 0.0 gives, are
   495	  neither of the two cases TI draws.
   496	### The TPAD via barrels are left open — and why the first fix was wrong
   497	
   498	Review finding C said the six thermal via barrels are open on the contact face.
   499	They were tented for one revision on three arguments. Only one survives:
   500	
   501	- *"Six holes through the insulating barrier"* — **wrong, and it was the argument
   502	  I leaned on hardest.** The barrel is on net `TPAD`, the same net as the pour
   503	  around it, so an open barrel exposes no new potential. And the barrier is the
   504	  TIM between shunt and board; a hole in the **board** does not breach it, it
   505	  only lets compound extrude into it.
   506	- *Solder escaping at reflow* — weak. The nearest paste is 0.30 mm away and not
   507	  in the pads, so little drives wicking.
   508	- *Grease pump-through* — real. Under clamp and thermal cycling, compound
   509	  migrates into six 0.3 mm holes and the bond line thins.
   510	
   511	The cost of tenting, which the first revision did not state: LPI mask stands
   512	10–25 µm proud, so it put a **5.51 mm²** plateau (not the ~3 mm² first written)
   513	about 20 µm high in the middle of a face that must sit flat against a shunt,
   514	directly over the vias — and dropped the contact area to 40.9 mm². With grease
   515	that is roughly neutral. With a solid TIM pad or a hard clamp it holds the board
   516	off the shunt at exactly the wrong place.
   517	
   518	**Decision: leave the barrels bare, no via plugging.** Flat face, full contact
   519	area (**45.9 mm²**), and the accepted cost is grease pump-through over time —
   520	acceptable for a bench probe that gets re-bedded between runs. Resin-filled +
   521	capped vias (IPC-4761 Type VII) would give flat *and* sealed, but only as a
   522	per-order JLCPCB option, which is a dependency that is easy to forget.
   523	
   524	`audit.py::bottom_mask_integrity` was reshaped to match: it gates **foreign-net**
   525	copper exposed in a bottom aperture (finding B) and reports the open TPAD barrels
   526	as a counted, accepted condition. It is not a mute button — a signal via drifting
   527	into the aperture still fails the build, and the guard fails rather than passes if
   528	it has no foreign-net via to measure against.
   529	
   530	**Enabling the rule immediately exposed a second, package-forced case.** At the
   531	TMP117's 0.65 mm pitch the mask dam between the exposed pad and each terminal
   532	land is 0.15 mm, below any fab minimum, so those apertures gang. That is normal
   533	on a fine-pitch no-lead package, and it is handled as a **scoped exemption, not a
   534	muted rule**: `allow_soldermask_bridges` is declared on the TMP117 *footprint*
   535	(it is a property of the package, so the board's copy stays identical to the
   536	library), and `audit.py::mask_bridge_scope` fails the build if any footprint
   537	other than U1 acquires it. The full 0.25 mm dam is still enforced everywhere
   538	else, including every bottom aperture.
   539	
   540	Two harness bugs surfaced while writing the guards, both worth recording:
   541	
   542	- The first `allow_mask_bridges()` used a regex pairing an `(attr …)` line with a
   543	  later `(property "Reference" …)`. Those spanned block boundaries, so the
   544	  exemption landed on **C1** and U1 got nothing — visible only as an unrelated
   545	  `lib_footprint_mismatch` on C1. Now walks balanced blocks and reads both
   546	  fields from inside the same one.
   547	- `bottom_mask_integrity()` first compared aperture bounding boxes in absolute
   548	  page coordinates against via positions in board-local ones. Every via came out
   549	  100 mm from every aperture, so none read as open and none read as close — and
   550	  the guard reported a very comfortable **133 mm of "mask web"**. A wrong answer
   551	  wearing a generous pass. Caught because the injection did not fire.
   552	
   553	### Reviewer claims I checked and did not accept
   554	
   555	- **"6 vias → ~65 K/W"** — the omission was real, the number was not. Measured 34 K/W by walking the copper graph.
   556	- **"contact area 50.2 mm² is 8 % inflated"** — correct in direction; measured 46.5 mm² against the reviewer's 46.29 mm².
   557	- **`_path_length()` is layer-blind** — flagged by inspection only; the reviewer could not build a demonstration and said so. Left open, unconfirmed.
